  "_comment": "E13 router probe (#464 follow-up, 2026-06-21). Tests whether a CHEAP query-time STRUCTURAL signal (compactness of top search hits over the related-graph) can auto-route between neighborhood/multihop queries (C wins) and scattered/reasoning queries (A wins). NEGATIVE RESULT: it cannot, on this graph. capture3 neighborhood mean 0.976 vs scattered 0.964 (no separation); largestClusterFrac=1.0 for ALL queries/classes; best threshold acc=0.60 = base rate. CAUSE (fundamental, not tuning): the 73-node/255-edge graph is too small+dense — avg node reaches 11%/53%/91% of the graph within 1/2/3 hops, so a hops=2 pack from the top-3 seeds already spans ~half the graph; k-hop locality localizes nothing. The neighborhood-vs-scattered distinction is NOT structural reachability (always true here) but lexical/semantic (gold near-but-off-seed) which no unsupervised signal sees without labels. Implication: no cheap auto-router at this scale (and it is unnecessary — the pack ~= half the graph anyway); routing falls to the AGENT reading query intent (already works) or default-on. A structural router might only become feasible+necessary on a MUCH larger graph (1000s of nodes) where 2-hop neighborhoods are genuinely local."
}
